{"id":"fbd24ca2-23b3-4b2b-8841-0c11b922d30f","object":"chat.completion","created":1785015816,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    import json\\n    # Validate top-level keys\\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\\n        raise ValueError('case must be a dict')\\n    for key in ('min_cluster_size', 'clusters', 'parent'):\\n        if key not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Missing key: {key}')\\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\\n    clusters_raw = case['clusters']\\n    parent_map = case['parent']\\n    if not isinstance(clusters_raw, list):\\n        raise ValueError('clusters must be a list')\\n    if not isinstance(parent_map, dict):\\n        raise ValueError('parent must be a dict')\\n    # Build lookup\\n    cluster_by_id = {}\\n    for cl in clusters_raw:\\n        if not isinstance(cl, dict):\\n            raise ValueError('each cluster must be a dict')\\n        cid = cl.get('cluster_id')\\n        if cid is None or not isinstance(cid, str):\\n            raise ValueError('cluster_id required as string')\\n        if 'size' not in cl or not isinstance(cl['size'], int):\\n            raise ValueError('size required as int')\\n        if 'birth_lambda' not in cl or not isinstance(cl['birth_lambda'], (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError('birth_lambda required as number')\\n        if 'point_exit_lambdas' not in cl or not isinstance(cl['point_exit_lambdas'], list):\\n            raise ValueError('point_exit_lambdas required as list')\\n        cluster_by_id[cid] = cl\\n    # Validate parent map\\n    for cid, pid in parent_map.items():\\n        if cid not in cluster_by_id:\\n            raise ValueError(f'parent map key {cid} not in clusters')\\n        if pid is not None and pid not in cluster_by_id:\\n            raise ValueError(f'parent map value {pid} for {cid} not in clusters')\\n    # Filter by min size\\n    retained = {cid: cluster_by_id[cid] for cid in cluster_by_id if cluster_by_id[cid]['size'] >= min_size}\\n    # Compute stability\\n    stability = {}\\n    for cid, cl in retained.items():\\n        birth = cl['birth_lambda']\\n        total = 0.0\\n        for exit_l in cl['point_exit_lambdas']:\\n            v = exit_l - birth\\n            if v > 0:\\n                total += v\\n        stability[cid] = total\\n    # Sort retained clusters: descending stability, then ascending cluster_id\\n    sorted_clusters = sorted(retained.keys(), key=lambda cid: (-stability[cid], cid))\\n    # Build child lists (retained only)\\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained}\\n    for cid in retained:\\n        pid = parent_map.get(cid)\\n        if pid is not None and pid in retained:\\n            children[pid].append(cid)\\n    # Ancestor chain helper\\n    def get_ancestors(cid):\\n        anc = []\\n        cur = cid\\n        while cur in parent_map and parent_map[cur] is not None:\\n            par = parent_map[cur]\\n            if par in retained:\\n                anc.append(par)\\n            cur = par\\n        return anc\\n    # Descendant check\\n    def has_selected_descendant(cid, selected_set):\\n        stack = [cid]\\n        while stack:\\n            node = stack.pop()\\n            for child in children.get(node, []):\\n                if child in selected_set:\\n                    return True\\n                stack.append(child)\\n        return False\\n    selected = []\\n    forbidden_ancestors = set()\\n    for cid in sorted_clusters:\\n        if cid in forbidden_ancestors:\\n            continue\\n        if has_selected_descendant(cid, set(selected)):\\n            continue\\n        selected.append(cid)\\n        for a in get_ancestors(cid):\\n            forbidden_ancestors.add(a)\\n    # Build output\\n    clusters_out = []\\n    for cid in sorted_clusters:\\n        clusters_out.append({\\n            'cluster_id': cid,\\n            'size': retained[cid]['size'],\\n            'stability': stability[cid]\\n        })\\n    selected.sort()\\n    return {\\n        'clusters': clusters_out,\\n        'selected_cluster_ids': selected\\n    }\\n\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that takes a payload (the inner object from fixture) and returns a JSON object with \"clusters\" array and \"selected_cluster_ids\" array.\n\nFrom the problem: \"condensed_tree_stability\" task: given a hierarchy with clusters, each has cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas (list), and parent mapping (cluster_id -> parent_id or null). Also min_cluster_size.\n\nSteps:\n1. Validate payload: must have min_cluster_size, clusters (list of objects), parent (dict). Each cluster must have cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas. Also perhaps parent keys must match cluster_ids.\n2. Filter clusters: keep those with size >= min_cluster_size.\n3. For each retained cluster, compute stability = sum over its point_exit_lambdas of max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). But careful: \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" So for each point in the cluster (point_exit_lambdas is list of exit lambdas for each point? The cluster has a single birth_lambda and multiple points each with an exit_lambda. So stability = sum(max(0, exit - birth) for exit in point_exit_lambdas).\n4. Then rank clusters by decreasing stability, and for ties, by increasing cluster_id? The spec: \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" That likely means sort by stability descending, and for equal stability, sort by cluster_id ascending (string comparison). But careful: \"cluster ID\" is a string, presumably can be compared lexicographically.\n5. Select clusters: iterate sorted list. For each cluster, if none of its descendants (in the original hierarchy, but after filtering? The \"descendants\" refer to clusters that are in the retained set and are descendants in the parent tree. Since we only consider retained clusters, we need to consider the tree among retained clusters. Typically, condensed tree selection: after pruning by min size, we have a tree (parent pointers). We select a cluster only if no already selected descendant blocks it (i.e., no selected cluster that is a descendant of this cluster). Then after selecting, block its ancestors (i.e., mark ancestors as not selectable). This is the standard algorithm: traverse in stability order, if a cluster's descendants (in the original tree) have not been selected, select it and then exclude all its ancestors from selection. But careful: \"no already selected descendant blocks it\" means if a descendant is already selected, then this cluster is blocked. Also after selecting, block its ancestors (so they cannot be selected later). This is typical for hierarchical cluster selection to avoid overlapping clusters.\n6. The output: \"clusters\" array with objects: cluster_id, size, stability (float), and \"selected_cluster_ids\" list of strings in sorted order? The output says selected_cluster_ids is an array of strings. The spec: \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" The selected_cluster_ids should be sorted? The output schema doesn't specify order but \"sorted selected IDs\" implies sorted. Probably ascending order. The clusters array can be in any order? Might be based on original order? But we can output in the order we computed (sorted by stability then id). That's fine.\n7. For descendant checking: we need to know which clusters are descendants in the original tree (parent mapping). Since we only have parent mapping (each cluster has a parent or null). So we can build a ancestry tree. For each cluster, we can find all descendants by traversing children. Or simpler: to check if a descendant is selected, we can iterate over selected IDs and check if any is a descendant of current cluster. Also to block ancestors: we need to know ancestors of current cluster. So build parent mapping and child mapping.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Input case: dictionary. Ensure it has keys: min_cluster_size, clusters, parent.\n- Validate that clusters list is non-empty? Probably not required, but handle gracefully.\n- For each cluster in clusters, check that it has cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas (list). Also size should be integer? The input has integers. stability returns number.\n- Build mapping from cluster_id to cluster dict.\n- Validate that parent keys are exactly cluster_ids? Not necessary but okay.\n- Filter: retain only clusters with size >= min_cluster_size.\n- Compute stability for each retained cluster.\n- Sort retained clusters by stability descending, then cluster_id ascending.\n- Build a set of retained cluster IDs for quick lookup.\n- For descendant/ancestor tracking: we need the original parent mapping, but only among retained clusters? The algorithm typically considers the whole original hierarchy, but if a descendant cluster is not retained (size < min), it is considered noise and does not block. So we only consider retained clusters. So we need to build a tree of retained clusters: for each retained cluster, its parent (if parent is also retained) else null. Then we can compute ancestors and descendants within retained tree.\n- To find descendants: recursively from current cluster, traverse children (based on parent mapping reversed). Since the tree is not necessarily binary, but we can build child list from parent mapping for retained clusters.\n- For selection: maintain a set selected_ids. Iterate sorted list. For each cluster:\n   - Check if any of its descendants (in retained tree) are already in selected_ids. If yes, skip (since descendant blocks it).\n   - Else, select it: add to selected_ids. Then block its ancestors: mark that all ancestors of this cluster (in retained tree) cannot be selected later. How to block? Instead of marking ancestors as \"blocked\", we can simply skip future clusters that are ancestors of selected clusters. Because we iterate in stability order, later clusters will be lower stability. So if we select a cluster, its ancestors (which appear later because they must have larger stability? Not necessarily; but we iterate descending stability, so ancestors could be before or after. Actually ancestors (closer to root) typically have larger stability? Not guaranteed. So we need to prevent selection of ancestors later. So after selecting, we can mark the ancestors as \"blocked\" (set). Then for each future cluster, we check if its ID is in blocked set; if so, skip. Also we need to consider that a cluster might be blocked because a descendant was selected, but also because an ancestor was selected? Actually the rule: select a cluster only if no already selected descendant blocks it. That means if a descendant is selected, this cluster is blocked. Also after selecting, we block its ancestors (so they cannot be selected). So the blocked set includes all ancestors of selected clusters. Additionally, if a cluster is an ancestor of a selected cluster, it will be blocked. But also if a cluster is a descendant? That would have been already considered because descendant would have been selected earlier (if it had higher stability) and thus blocks this cluster. So the condition: a cluster can be selected if none of its descendants are selected and it is not already blocked as an ancestor of some selected cluster. Actually the blocking of ancestors after selection ensures that no ancestor is selected later. So we can maintain a set `forbidden` for IDs that are ancestors of already selected clusters. Then when iterating, for each cluster, if cluster_id in forbidden: skip. Else, check if any descendant is selected (by checking if any selected_descendant exists). If no descendant selected, select it and add all its ancestors to forbidden.\n\n- Implementation of descendants: we can compute descendants once for each cluster? Or compute on the fly using a recursive function that traverses children (precomputed child list). Since the tree size is small (fixture has 3 clusters), any approach is fine. But we should be efficient.\n\n- For ancestors: we need to get the chain of ancestors up to root (only retained). We can precompute parent mapping for retained clusters.\n\n- Edge cases: clusters with no parent (null). Root cluster. If multiple roots? Possibly.\n\n- After selection, we also need to output clusters for all retained clusters (including those not selected) with stability values. The output schema requires \"clusters\" array with all retained clusters (size and stability). The selected_cluster_ids is sorted (ascending). So we can compute clusters list as we have from retained set, maybe in the order we computed (sorted by stability then id) but that's fine.\n\n- Validate: raise ValueError for infeasible or invalid case. For example, missing required fields, clusters not having required fields, parent mapping referencing non-existent cluster, etc.\n\nNow, write Python code.\n\nWe need to return a JSON serializable dict with \"clusters\" and \"selected_cluster_ids\".\n\nLet me code solve(case):\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate\n    if not isinstance(case, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"case must be a dict\")\n    for key in ('min_cluster_size', 'clusters', 'parent'):\n        if key not in case:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Missing key: {key}\")\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\n    clusters_raw = case['clusters']\n    parent_map = case['parent']\n    if not isinstance(clusters_raw, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"clusters must be a list\")\n    if not isinstance(parent_map, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"parent must be a dict\")\n    # Build cluster dict\n    cluster_by_id = {}\n    for cl in clusters_raw:\n        if not isinstance(cl, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"each cluster must be a dict\")\n        cid = cl.get('cluster_id')\n        if cid is None or not isinstance(cid, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"cluster_id required as string\")\n        if 'size' not in cl or not isinstance(cl['size'], int):\n            raise ValueError(\"size required as int\")\n        if 'birth_lambda' not in cl or not isinstance(cl['birth_lambda'], (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"birth_lambda required as number\")\n        if 'point_exit_lambdas' not in cl or not isinstance(cl['point_exit_lambdas'], list):\n            raise ValueError(\"point_exit_lambdas required as list\")\n        cluster_by_id[cid] = cl\n    # Validate parent keys\n    for cid, pid in parent_map.items():\n        if cid not in cluster_by_id:\n            raise ValueError(f\"parent map key {cid} not in clusters\")\n        if pid is not None and pid not in cluster_by_id:\n            raise ValueError(f\"parent map value {pid} for {cid} not in clusters\")\n    # Filter by min size\n    retained = {cid: cluster_by_id[cid] for cid in cluster_by_id if cluster_by_id[cid]['size'] >= min_size}\n    # Compute stability for each retained cluster\n    stability = {}\n    for cid, cl in retained.items():\n        birth = cl['birth_lambda']\n        exits = cl['point_exit_lambdas']\n        total = 0.0\n        for exit_l in exits:\n            v = exit_l - birth\n            if v > 0:\n                total += v\n        stability[cid] = total\n    # Sort retained clusters by stability desc, then cluster_id asc\n    sorted_clusters = sorted(retained.keys(), key=lambda cid: (-stability[cid], cid))\n    # Build child list for retained clusters\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained}\n    for cid in retained:\n        pid = parent_map.get(cid) # parent may be None or not retained\n        if pid is not None and pid in retained:\n            children[pid].append(cid)\n    # Also build ancestor chain function using parent_map (but only retained ancestors)\n    def get_ancestors(cid):\n        anc = []\n        cur = cid\n        while cur in parent_map and parent_map[cur] is not None:\n            par = parent_map[cur]\n            if par in retained:\n                anc.append(par)\n            cur = par\n        return anc\n    # Precompute descendants? We'll do on the fly for each candidate\n    def has_selected_descendant(cid, selected_set):\n        # DFS from cid in children tree, check if any descendant in selected_set\n        stack = [cid]\n        while stack:\n            node = stack.pop()\n            # skip self in check? We want descendants only, so we don't check self.\n            # Actually we want to check if any descendant (child, grandchild, etc.) is selected.\n            # So we start with children.\n            for child in children.get(node, []):\n                if child in selected_set:\n                    return True\n                stack.append(child)\n        return False\n    selected = []\n    forbidden_ancestors = set()\n    # Iterate sorted list\n    for cid in sorted_clusters:\n        if cid in forbidden_ancestors:\n            continue\n        if has_selected_descendant(cid, selected_set=set(selected)):\n            continue\n        # select it\n        selected.append(cid)\n        # block its ancestors\n        anc = get_ancestors(cid)\n        for a in anc:\n            forbidden_ancestors.add(a)\n    # Now construct output\n    clusters_out = []\n    for cid in sorted_clusters:  # order from sorted list is fine\n        clusters_out.append({\n            \"cluster_id\": cid,\n            \"size\": retained[cid]['size'],\n            \"stability\": stability[cid]\n        })\n    selected.sort()  # sort ascending\n    output = {\n        \"clusters\": clusters_out,\n        \"selected_cluster_ids\": selected\n    }\n    return output\n\nCheck: The output schema requires \"clusters\" array with objects having cluster_id, size, stability. \"selected_cluster_ids\" array of strings.\n\nEdge: if no clusters retained (all below min_size), then sorted_clusters empty, selected empty, clusters_out empty. That